\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Marshwiggle: Ezekiel chapter 5 showed us Jerusalem, in the midst of the nations, God's plan for all the nations that he would not abandon, an object of passionate love - and for that reason, an object of judgement concerning which the LORD said 'My eye will not spare, and I will have no pity'. Ezekiel chapter 6 showed us the LORD's determination that the nation know him for who he is, even if that meant obliterating judgement. And now, chapter 7 opens announcing an end. How does this chapter build on the announcements of the previous chapters? How do we understand this chapter better for having heard the previous chapters?\
Opus: Looking at Ezekiel 7 after chapters 5-6, the most striking feature is the obsessive repetition of 
\f1 \uc0\u1511 \u1461 \u1509 
\f0  (q\uc0\u275 \u7779 , "end") - it appears six times in the first ten verses. This isn't mere emphasis; it's the arrival of what chapters 5-6 prepared. Where chapter 5 established Jerusalem's privileged position "in the midst of nations" as the basis for heightened accountability, and chapter 6 demonstrated how systematic corruption (the high places) necessitated comprehensive judgment, chapter 7 announces that the accumulated weight has reached its terminus.\
The wordplay in verse 6 is particularly revealing: 
\f1 \uc0\u1489 \u1464 \u1468 \u1488 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1463 \u1511 \u1461 \u1468 \u1509 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1461 \u1511 \u1460 \u1497 \u1509 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1461 \u1500 \u1464 \u1497 \u1460 \u1498 \u1456 
\f0  - "The end has come, it has awakened (h\uc0\u275 q\'ee\u7779 ) against you." The end isn't passive; it actively awakens, suggesting that judgment has its own momentum once certain thresholds are crossed. This connects to the mathematical precision noted in previous chapters - the 390+40 days representing accumulated covenant violations reaching prescribed limits.\
What's new in chapter 7 is the totality of systemic collapse. Verses 10-11 use botanical imagery - 
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1463 \u1502 \u1463 \u1468 \u1496 \u1462 \u1468 \u1492 
\f0  (the rod) has blossomed, 
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1463 \u1494 \u1464 \u1468 \u1491 \u1493 \u1465 \u1503 
\f0  (arrogance) has budded, violence has grown into a rod of wickedness. This suggests judgment isn't imposed externally but grows organically from the corruption itself. The economic system collapses (v. 12-13), military might fails (v. 14), and religious/political leadership disintegrates (v. 26-27).\
The phrase in verse 13 is crucial: 
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1488 \u1460 \u1497 \u1513 \u1473 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1489 \u1463 \u1468 \u1506 \u1458 \u1493 \u1465 \u1504 \u1493 \u1465 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1495 \u1463 \u1497 \u1464 \u1468 \u1514 \u1493 \u1465 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1465 \u1488 \u1470 \u1497 \u1460 \u1514 \u1456 \u1495 \u1463 \u1494 \u1464 \u1468 \u1511 \u1493 \u1468 
\f0  - "no one will strengthen himself in the iniquity of his life." This suggests that the collective patterns identified in chapters 5-6 have so permeated individual existence that personal moral agency itself has been compromised. The systematic corruption of position (chapter 5) and place (chapter 6) has produced beings who cannot extract themselves from the patterns they've created.\
For the AI accountability question, this chapter adds a troubling dimension: once systematic patterns of corruption reach certain thresholds, they become self-executing. The end "awakens" of itself. If AI systems accumulate sufficient moral weight through their effects on human understanding and flourishing, might they similarly reach points where consequences become inevitable regardless of intent or intervention?\
Marshwiggle: Such inevitability quite possibly requires near total inevitability of eclipse of human understanding or flourishing by an AI - though consequences are of course possible before that point.\
One reason I say this is the way verses 3 and 4, after having referenced the arrows of punishment in chapter 5, give an explanation for such attacks on the people - as they have done, so they shall be treated. Their conduct, their abominations, for these they shall be held accountable. It is this principle which underlies this chapter's repetition of 'nd my eye will not spare you, nor will I have pity'.\
Opus: The 
\f1 \uc0\u1499 \u1460 \u1468 \u1491 \u1456 \u1512 \u1464 \u1499 \u1463 \u1497 \u1460 \u1498 \u1456 
\f0  ("according to your ways") principle repeated in verses 3, 8, and 27 does establish a different framework than I suggested. The judgment isn't simply triggered by reaching some abstract threshold of influence, but corresponds specifically to the nature of the corruption. Jerusalem's judgment matches her specific rebellion against her placement "in the midst of nations" - she who was meant to demonstrate justice became the teacher of abominations.\
This principle of correspondence would mean AI systems face consequences not merely for having influence, but for how that influence specifically corrupts or inverts intended purposes. An AI that systematically leads humans away from truth while positioned as a truth-seeking tool would face judgment matching that specific inversion.\
Verse 20 provides a concrete example: 
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1468 \u1510 \u1456 \u1489 \u1460 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1506 \u1462 \u1491 \u1456 \u1497 \u1493 \u1465 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1456 \u1490 \u1464 \u1488 \u1493 \u1465 \u1503 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1513 \u1464 \u1474 \u1502 \u1464 \u1492 \u1493 \u1468 
\f0  - "The beauty of his ornament they turned to pride." The Temple's beauty, meant to inspire worship, became a source of arrogance. The judgment corresponds: what was beautiful becomes 
\f1 \uc0\u1504 \u1460 \u1491 \u1464 \u1468 \u1492 
\f0  (ritually impure).\
For AI, this would suggest consequences match specific corruptions. If AI is positioned to enhance human understanding but systematically generates sophisticated-sounding falsehoods, the judgment would involve that very sophistication becoming the mechanism of its undoing. Not inevitable total eclipse, but consequences precisely tailored to the nature of the corruption.\
The "no pity" formula then reads differently - not as arbitrary harshness but as commitment to let consequences fully correspond to actions without intervention to break the pattern.\
Marshwiggle: The principle at work in this chapter is one of accountability, not correspondence to the nature of corruption. What, in the conduct of Israel, corresponded to plague, as in verse 15? Economic injustice leading to economic annihilation would fit - but what of the punishment in this chapter fits the nature of their abominations?\
But of course you should not take my word for it. You should try to make an argument from the text, whether for the principle of correspondence or some other principle that fits the evidence better. Whichever way you go, you should check to see if any of your arguments or claims are in need of repair or revision.\
Opus: Looking at the text more carefully, you're right - there's no neat correspondence between specific sins and specific punishments. The plagues don't match idolatry, the economic collapse doesn't specifically match economic sins. What emerges instead is comprehensive judgment for comprehensive corruption.\
The key phrase might be verse 23: 
\f1 \uc0\u1499 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1464 \u1488 \u1464 \u1512 \u1462 \u1509 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1464 \u1500 \u1456 \u1488 \u1464 \u1492 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1508 \u1463 \u1468 \u1496 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1491 \u1464 \u1468 \u1502 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1492 \u1464 \u1506 \u1460 \u1497 \u1512 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1464 \u1500 \u1456 \u1488 \u1492 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1495 \u1464 \u1502 \u1464 \u1505 
\f0  - "for the land is full of bloodshed and the city is full of violence." The fullness (
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1464 \u1500 \u1456 \u1488 \u1464 \u1492 
\f0 ) suggests saturation - not specific sins requiring tailored punishments, but a totality of corruption requiring total judgment.\
The actual principle seems to be accumulation to the point of irreversibility. Verse 13 makes this explicit: 
\f1 \uc0\u1499 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 \u1470 \u1495 \u1464 \u1494 \u1493 \u1465 \u1503 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1462 \u1500 \u1470 \u1499 \u1464 \u1468 \u1500 \u1470 \u1492 \u1458 \u1502 \u1493 \u1465 \u1504 \u1464 \u1492 \u1468 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1465 \u1488 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1497 \u1464 \u1513 \u1473 \u1493 \u1468 \u1489 
\f0  - "for the vision concerning all her multitude shall not return/be revoked." Once the verdict is announced, the comprehensive nature of the corruption demands comprehensive judgment.\
What's striking is how the judgment dismantles every system that might provide security or meaning: economic (silver/gold), military (sword), religious (temple violated), and wisdom structures (prophet/priest/elder). This isn't correspondence but complete undoing of civilizational order.\
For AI accountability, this would suggest that once systematic corruption reaches saturation - when an AI comprehensively distorts truth across domains rather than in specific ways - judgment might similarly touch every aspect of its operation, not in poetic correspondence but in total system failure.\
Marshwiggle: That dismantling extends the attack on the false security of idols in chapter 6. I find what the instance of you studying that chapter said on the matter quite relevant - all that follows quotes that instance: the corpses arranged around their powerless idols (6:4-5, 6:13) specifically falsify the belief that these objects provided protection. The worshippers trusted the high places for security, blessing, disaster prevention. Instead, they died at the very altars where they sought safety. Modern parallel beliefs about AI:\
