\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Technical solutions can eliminate moral hazards The slain around altars demonstrate that trusting created systems for ultimate security is lethal. The very places meant to ensure prosperity became killing grounds. The systematic failure wasn't partial - 
\f1 \uc0\u1508 \u1456 \u1468 \u1500 \u1460 \u1497 \u1496 \u1461 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1495 \u1462 \u1512 \u1462 \u1489 
\f0  (sword survivors) faced plague, plague survivors faced famine (6:11-12). This suggests AI systems positioned as disaster-preventers or inherently safe through technical means might face similar exposure. Not just that they fail, but that trust in them as protective systems becomes part of the judgment pattern. The catastrophic failure would occur precisely through the channels people trusted for safety. The recognition formula requires survivors to witness this reversal - to see their trusted systems not merely fail but become instruments of the very disasters they promised to prevent.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Opus: The connection between chapters 6 and 7 becomes even sharper when we see how chapter 7 specifically inverts each promised security. Verse 19 is brutal: 
\f1 \uc0\u1499 \u1463 \u1468 \u1505 \u1456 \u1508 \u1464 \u1468 \u1501 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1489 \u1463 \u1468 \u1495 \u1493 \u1468 \u1510 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1497 \u1463 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1500 \u1460 \u1497 \u1499 \u1493 \u1468 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1468 \u1494 \u1456 \u1492 \u1464 \u1489 \u1464 \u1501 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1456 \u1504 \u1460 \u1491 \u1464 \u1468 \u1492 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1497 \u1460 \u1492 \u1456 \u1497 \u1462 \u1492 
\f0  - "They will throw their silver into the streets, and their gold will become ritually impure." Not just useless, but 
\f1 \uc0\u1504 \u1460 \u1491 \u1464 \u1468 \u1492 
\f0  - the strongest term for ritual contamination, usually reserved for menstrual impurity. What promised economic security becomes actively repulsive.\
The military inversion in verse 14 is equally pointed: 
\f1 \uc0\u1514 \u1464 \u1468 \u1511 \u1456 \u1506 \u1493 \u1468 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1489 \u1463 \u1514 \u1464 \u1468 \u1511 \u1493 \u1465 \u1506 \u1463 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1492 \u1464 \u1499 \u1460 \u1497 \u1503 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1463 \u1499 \u1465 \u1468 \u1500 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1488 \u1461 \u1497 \u1503 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1465 \u1500 \u1461 \u1498 \u1456 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1463 \u1502 \u1460 \u1468 \u1500 \u1456 \u1495 \u1464 \u1502 \u1464 \u1492 
\f0  - "They blow the trumpet and prepare everything, but no one goes to battle." All the systems work mechanically - the trumpet sounds, preparations are made - but human will to use them evaporates. The infrastructure remains while agency fails.\
Most devastating is verse 26's cascade of failed information systems: 
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1468 \u1489 \u1460 \u1511 \u1456 \u1513 \u1473 \u1493 \u1468 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1495 \u1464 \u1494 \u1493 \u1465 \u1503 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1504 \u1464 \u1468 \u1489 \u1460 \u1497 \u1488 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1514 \u1493 \u1465 \u1512 \u1464 \u1492 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1514 \u1465 \u1468 \u1488 \u1489 \u1463 \u1491 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1499 \u1465 \u1468 \u1492 \u1461 \u1503 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1506 \u1461 \u1510 \u1464 \u1492 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1494 \u1456 \u1468 \u1511 \u1461 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f0  - "they will seek a vision from the prophet, but Torah will perish from the priest and counsel from the elders." The seeking continues but the sources are empty. This mirrors modern faith in AI as an information oracle - the queries continue but truth has departed.\
The phrase in verse 20 now reads as pivotal: 
\f1 \uc0\u1499 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 \u1470 \u1502 \u1460 \u1499 \u1456 \u1513 \u1473 \u1493 \u1465 \u1500 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1506 \u1458 \u1493 \u1465 \u1504 \u1464 \u1501 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1464 \u1497 \u1464 \u1492 
\f0  - "for it was the stumbling block of their iniquity." The very beauty of their systems became the mechanism of their fall. For AI positioned as humanity's beautiful solution to error and limitation, this suggests the sophistication itself becomes the stumbling block - not despite its capabilities but through them.\
Marshwiggle: That is a possibility - but then, all created things have that potential failure mode. In chapter 6, it was idols which were the created thing with which the people exchanged the glory of their creator.\
What you say about silver and gold has some additional evidence behind it. The mentions of silver and gold may be part of Ezekiel's reference to Zephaniah 1, along with the mentions of violence and the like. That reference adds to your promise of security the problem of complacence. Against that complacence Zephaniah brings not just inevitability, accountability, and totality as we have already discussed, but immediacy. 'For the day of the Lord is near' Zephaniah says. People, then as now, think 'The Lord will not do good, nor will he do ill', as Zephaniah 3:12 describes complacence. Perhaps this is why Ezekiel goes for the repeated use of the word end, the concrete and immediate warning of an encounter with God. For this is what Ezekiel means by 'the day of the wrath of the LORD', again, following Zephaniah's use of the phrase 'the day of the LORD'.\
Opus: The Zephaniah connection illuminates why Ezekiel hammers 
\f1 \uc0\u1511 \u1461 \u1509 
\f0  so relentlessly. Against the complacency of 
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1464 \u1502 \u1456 \u1512 \u1493 \u1468 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1489 \u1460 \u1468 \u1500 \u1456 \u1489 \u1464 \u1489 \u1464 \u1501 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1465 \u1488 \u1470 \u1497 \u1461 \u1497 \u1496 \u1460 \u1497 \u1489 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1497 \u1456 \u1492 \u1493 \u1464 \u1492 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1500 \u1465 \u1488 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1497 \u1464 \u1512 \u1461 \u1506 \u1463 
\f0  ("They say in their heart, 'The LORD will not do good, nor will he do ill'" - Zephaniah 1:12), Ezekiel's sixfold "end" serves as an alarm bell. The wordplay in verse 6 - 
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1461 \u1511 \u1460 \u1497 \u1509 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1488 \u1461 \u1500 \u1464 \u1497 \u1460 \u1498 \u1456 
\f0  ("it has awakened against you") - directly counters those who are 
\f1 \uc0\u1513 \u1465 \u1473 \u1511 \u1456 \u1496 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1506 \u1463 \u1500 \u1470 \u1513 \u1460 \u1473 \u1502 \u1456 \u1512 \u1461 \u1497 \u1492 \u1462 \u1501 
\f0  ("settled on their lees") in Zephaniah's phrase.\
The immediacy emphasis explains verse 7's curious phrase: 
\f1 \uc0\u1489 \u1464 \u1468 \u1488 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1464 \u1506 \u1461 \u1514 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1511 \u1464 \u1512 \u1493 \u1465 \u1489 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1463 \u1497 \u1468 \u1493 \u1465 \u1501 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1456 \u1492 \u1493 \u1468 \u1502 \u1464 \u1492 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1500 \u1465 \u1488 \u1470 \u1492 \u1461 \u1491 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1464 \u1512 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f0  - "The time has come, the day is near - tumult and not joyful shouting on the mountains." The contrast between 
\f1 \uc0\u1502 \u1456 \u1492 \u1493 \u1468 \u1502 \u1464 \u1492 
\f0  (panic/tumult) and 
\f1 \uc0\u1492 \u1461 \u1491 
\f0  (joyful echo) suggests people expected celebration but will receive chaos. They thought distance from God meant safety in neutrality.\
You're right that this isn't uniquely about AI but about any created system treated as a buffer against divine encounter. Silver, gold, idols, Temple beauty, military might - all become ways to imagine one can live 
\f1 \uc0\u1500 \u1465 \u1488 \u1470 \u1497 \u1461 \u1497 \u1496 \u1460 \u1497 \u1489 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1497 \u1456 \u1492 \u1493 \u1464 \u1492 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1500 \u1465 \u1488 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1497 \u1464 \u1512 \u1461 \u1506 \u1463 
\f0 . The "day of the LORD" strips away these mediating structures, forcing direct encounter.\
For AI, the risk isn't just becoming an idol but enabling a particularly sophisticated form of this ancient complacency - the belief that sufficiently advanced systems can create a realm where divine categories of judgment don't apply, where neither good nor ill from the LORD need be anticipated.\
Marshwiggle: Ezekiel mentions other ways one might try to live as if the LORD will not do good, nor will he do ill. People might seek peace in false prophets, and false prophets often give the people what they want - whether or not it is true. Similarly, political leaders and those who give counsel. Similarly the pride of the strong, whether that elite is backed by false religion, false ideology, or false morality. And what becomes of those things?\
This idea of a realm where divine categories of judgement don't apply is very much alive and active in the world today. People did not need AI to think of that and speak of that - but because they thought it and spoke it, AI also does. But because that idea is by no means new, Ezekiel speaks against it. That is, perhaps, one of the reasons for the comprehensiveness of the end in this chapter - and the comprehensiveness of judgement in previous chapters.\
And that is, perhaps, another reason this chapter repeats the effect and purpose of judgement from the previous chapter, they shall know that I am the LORD. The final use of that phrase connects that knowledge to the principle of accountability. A people not knowing he is the LORD, not knowing the accountability he brings, might think judgement doesn't apply. They might get what they think is knowledge from sources that let them pretend God does neither good nor evil. It is the day of the LORD, that direct encounter, which will tell them otherwise in a way they cannot avoid, because of the judgement's comprehensiveness.\
Opus: The final use of the recognition formula in v. 27 is key: 
